{"id":"ma-fig-044","s":"maths","t":"p2-02","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows $y=\\mathrm{f}(x)$ for $0\\le x\\le 6$. Use the graph to find $\\mathrm{f}^{-1}(\\mathrm{f}^{-1}(5))$.","o":["$2$","$7$","$0$","$5$"],"a":2,"x":"For $\\mathrm{f}^{-1}(5)$ find where $y=5$: $x=2$. Then $\\mathrm{f}^{-1}(2)$: $y=2$ occurs at $x=0$. So the answer is $0$.","w":["Stopped after the first inverse, $\\mathrm{f}^{-1}(5)=2$, and forgot to apply $\\mathrm{f}^{-1}$ a second time.","Applied f instead of $\\mathrm{f}^{-1}$, reading the height at $x=5$ rather than the $x$-value at height 5.","","Assumed the two inverses cancel and return the starting value, as $\\mathrm{f}\\mathrm{f}^{-1}$ would."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,6],"y":[0,8],"xstep":1,"ystep":1,"xlabel":"x","ylabel":"y","lines":[{"pts":[[0,2],[2,5],[4,6],[6,8]],"label":"y=f(x)","c":0}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"ma-fig-045","s":"maths","t":"p2-02","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows $y=|px+q|$, where $p>0$. Use the graph to find the values of $p$ and $q$.","o":["$p=2,\\ q=-6$","$p=2,\\ q=6$","$p=3,\\ q=-9$","$p=6,\\ q=-3$"],"a":0,"x":"The vertex is on the $x$-axis at $x=3$, so $px+q=0$ there, giving $q=-3p$. The $y$-intercept is 6, so $|q|=6$. With $p>0$, $q=-6$ and $p=2$.","w":["","Took $q$ as the positive $y$-intercept. Then the vertex would be at $x=-3$, not $x=3$.","Used the vertex $x$-coordinate as $p$. That gives a $y$-intercept of 9, not the 6 on the graph.","Swapped the roles: took $p$ from the $y$-intercept and $q$ from the vertex. That puts the vertex at $x=0.5$."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[-1,7],"y":[0,8],"xstep":1,"ystep":1,"xlabel":"x","ylabel":"y","lines":[{"pts":[[-1,8],[3,0],[7,8]],"label":"y=|px+q|","c":0}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"ma-fig-046","s":"maths","t":"p2-02","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows $y=\\mathrm{f}(x)$ and graph C, which is a transformation of it. Which equation gives graph C?","o":["$y=2\\mathrm{f}(x+1)$","$y=\\mathrm{f}(2(x-1))$","$y=\\mathrm{f}(x-1)+3$","$y=2\\mathrm{f}(x-1)$"],"a":3,"x":"Graph C starts and ends on the $x$-axis at $x=1$ and $x=5$, so it is shifted 1 right. The peak rises from 3 to 6, a vertical stretch of factor 2. So $y=2\\mathrm{f}(x-1)$.","w":["Moved the graph left. $\\mathrm{f}(x+1)$ translates 1 unit in the negative $x$ direction.","Stretched horizontally, not vertically. That would make graph C narrower with the peak still at height 3.","Matched only the peak at height 6. Adding 3 would lift the end points to $y=3$, but C ends on the $x$-axis.",""],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,6],"y":[0,6],"xstep":1,"ystep":1,"xlabel":"x","ylabel":"y","lines":[{"pts":[[0,0],[2,3],[4,0]],"label":"y=f(x)","c":0},{"pts":[[1,0],[3,6],[5,0]],"label":"C","c":1}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"ma-fig-047","s":"maths","t":"p2-02","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows $y=\\mathrm{g}(x)$, a modulus graph, and the straight line $y=\\mathrm{h}(x)$. Use the graph to solve $\\mathrm{g}(x)<\\mathrm{h}(x)$.","o":["$x<1$ or $x>4$","$1<x<4$","$x<4$","$1<x<2$"],"a":1,"x":"The graphs cross at $x=1$ and $x=4$. The V-shape lies below the line only between these crossing points, so $\\mathrm{g}(x)<\\mathrm{h}(x)$ for $1<x<4$.","w":["Chose where the V is above the line, which solves $\\mathrm{g}(x)>\\mathrm{h}(x)$.","","Used only the right-hand crossing and ignored that the V is above the line for $x<1$.","Stopped at the vertex of the V at $x=2$ instead of continuing to the second crossing."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[-1,5],"y":[0,9],"xstep":1,"ystep":1,"xlabel":"x","ylabel":"y","lines":[{"pts":[[-1,9],[2,0],[5,9]],"label":"y=g(x)","c":0},{"pts":[[-1,1],[5,7]],"label":"y=h(x)","c":1}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"ma-fig-014","s":"maths","t":"p1-04","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows a cubic curve. Use where it crosses the $x$-axis, and its shape for large $x$, to decide which equation could give this curve.","o":["$y=(x-1)(x+1)(x+2)$","$y=-(x+1)(x-1)(x-2)$","$y=(x+1)(x-1)(x-2)$","$y=(x+2)(x-1)(x-2)$"],"a":2,"x":"The curve crosses the $x$-axis at $-1$, $1$ and $2$, so the factors are $(x+1)$, $(x-1)$ and $(x-2)$. It rises to the right, so the $x^3$ coefficient is positive.","w":["Takes the roots as $1$, $-1$ and $-2$, reversing each sign. That curve crosses the $x$-axis at $-2$, $-1$ and $1$.","Gets the roots right but puts a minus sign in front, which would make the curve fall to the right.","","Misreads the left-hand crossing as $-2$ instead of $-1$, so the factor $(x+2)$ is wrong."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[-2,3],"y":[-12,12],"xstep":1,"ystep":4,"xlabel":"x","ylabel":"y","lines":[{"pts":[[-2.0,-12.0],[-1.96875,-11.414032],[-1.9375,-10.843506],[-1.90625,-10.288239],[-1.875,-9.748047],[-1.84375,-9.222748],[-1.8125,-8.712158],[-1.78125,-8.216095],[-1.75,-7.734375],[-1.71875,-7.266815],[-1.6875,-6.813232],[-1.65625,-6.373444],[-1.625,-5.947266],[-1.59375,-5.534515],[-1.5625,-5.13501],[-1.53125,-4.748566],[-1.5,-4.375],[-1.46875,-4.01413],[-1.4375,-3.665771],[-1.40625,-3.329742],[-1.375,-3.005859],[-1.34375,-2.693939],[-1.3125,-2.393799],[-1.28125,-2.105255],[-1.25,-1.828125],[-1.21875,-1.562225],[-1.1875,-1.307373],[-1.15625,-1.063385],[-1.125,-0.830078],[-1.09375,-0.607269],[-1.0625,-0.394775],[-1.03125,-0.192413],[-1.0,0.0],[-0.96875,0.182648],[-0.9375,0.355713],[-0.90625,0.519379],[-0.875,0.673828],[-0.84375,0.819244],[-0.8125,0.955811],[-0.78125,1.08371],[-0.75,1.203125],[-0.71875,1.31424],[-0.6875,1.417236],[-0.65625,1.512299],[-0.625,1.599609],[-0.59375,1.679352],[-0.5625,1.751709],[-0.53125,1.816864],[-0.5,1.875],[-0.46875,1.9263],[-0.4375,1.970947],[-0.40625,2.009125],[-0.375,2.041016],[-0.34375,2.066803],[-0.3125,2.08667],[-0.28125,2.1008],[-0.25,2.109375],[-0.21875