{"id":"ma-fig-039","s":"maths","t":"m1-10","lv":2,"q":"A lift of mass 500 kg is raised by a cable. The graph shows its upward velocity. Take $g=9.8$. Find the tension in the cable during the final stage, when the lift is slowing down.","o":["$5900$ N","$3900$ N","$4900$ N","$4650$ N"],"a":1,"x":"Final stage: velocity falls from 8 to 0 m/s in 4 s, so $a=-2$ m/s². Upwards: $T-500g=500\\times(-2)$, so $T=4900-1000=3900$ N.","w":["Gives the tension while speeding up. The deceleration is added to the weight instead of subtracted.","","Treats the final stage as constant velocity, so tension equals weight and the gradient is ignored.","Uses the gradient upside down, giving $a=4/8=0.5$ instead of 2, then $T=500(9.8-0.5)$."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,14],"y":[0,10],"xstep":2,"ystep":2,"xlabel":"time / s","ylabel":"velocity / m s⁻¹","lines":[{"pts":[[0,0],[4,8],[10,8],[14,0]],"c":0}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"ma-fig-040","s":"maths","t":"m1-10","lv":3,"q":"A block is pulled along a rough horizontal surface by a horizontal force $F$. The graph shows its acceleration against $F$. Find the mass of the block and the constant resistance to its motion.","o":["$6$ kg and $6$ N","$0.33$ kg and $6$ N","$3$ kg and $6$ N","$3$ kg and $2$ N"],"a":2,"x":"$F-R=ma$, so $a=\\frac{1}{m}F-\\frac{R}{m}$. The line leaves the axis at $F=6$, so $R=6$ N. Gradient $=\\frac{6-2}{24-12}=\\frac13=\\frac1m$, so $m=3$ kg.","w":["Uses $m=F/a$ at $F=12$, $a=2$, ignoring the resistance. The gradient gives $1/m$, not $F/a$.","Takes the gradient $\\frac13$ as the mass itself. The gradient is $1/m$, so the mass is its reciprocal.","","Reads the extended line's intercept on the acceleration axis (−2) as the resistance. The resistance is the force where $a=0$."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,24],"y":[0,6],"xstep":3,"ystep":1,"xlabel":"force F / N","ylabel":"acceleration / m s⁻²","lines":[{"pts":[[0,0],[6,0],[24,6]],"c":0}],"points":[{"at":[12,2],"label":"P"},{"at":[24,6],"label":"Q"}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"ma-fig-025","s":"maths","t":"p1-13","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows $y=x^2-6x+8$, crossing the $x$-axis at A and B. Find the area of the finite region between the curve and the $x$-axis, from A to B.","o":["$-\\frac{4}{3}$","$\\frac{4}{3}$","$\\frac{16}{3}$","$\\frac{20}{3}$"],"a":1,"x":"A and B are at $x=2$ and $x=4$. $\\int_2^4(x^2-6x+8)\\,dx=\\left[\\frac{x^3}{3}-3x^2+8x\\right]_2^4=-\\frac43$. The curve is below the axis, so the area is $\\frac43$.","w":["The integral is negative because the curve is below the axis. Area must be positive, so the sign has to be changed.","","Used limits $0$ and $4$ instead of the roots at A and B, reading the y-intercept side of the graph.","Used limits $0$ and $2$, taking the origin as the start instead of A at $x=2$."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,6],"y":[-2,8],"xstep":1,"ystep":2,"xlabel":"x","ylabel":"y","lines":[{"pts":[[0.0,8.0],[0.0375,7.776406],[0.075,7.555625],[0.1125,7.337656],[0.15,7.1225],[0.1875,6.910156],[0.225,6.700625],[0.2625,6.493906],[0.3,6.29],[0.3375,6.088906],[0.375,5.890625],[0.4125,5.695156],[0.45,5.5025],[0.4875,5.312656],[0.525,5.125625],[0.5625,4.941406],[0.6,4.76],[0.6375,4.581406],[0.675,4.405625],[0.7125,4.232656],[0.75,4.0625],[0.7875,3.895156],[0.825,3.730625],[0.8625,3.568906],[0.9,3.41],[0.9375,3.253906],[0.975,3.100625],[1.0125,2.950156],[1.05,2.8025],[1.0875,2.657656],[1.125,2.515625],[1.1625,2.376406],[1.2,2.24],[1.2375,2.106406],[1.275,1.975625],[1.3125,1.847656],[1.35,1.7225],[1.3875,1.600156],[1.425,1.480625],[1.4625,1.363906],[1.5,1.25],[1.5375,1.138906],[1.575,1.030625],[1.6125,0.925156],[1.65,0.8225],[1.6875,0.722656],[1.725,0.625625],[1.7625,0.531406],[1.8,0.44],[1.8375,0.351406],[1.875,0.265625],[1.9125,0.182656],[1.95,0.1025],[1.9875,0.025156],[2.025,-0.049375],[2.0625,-0.121094],[2.1,-0.19],[2.1375,-0.256094],[2.175,-0.319375],[2.2125,-0.379844],[2.25,-0.4375],[2.2875,-0.492344],[2.325,-0.544375],[2.3625,-0.593594],[2.4,-0.64],[2.4375,-0.683594],[2.475,-0.724375],[2.5125,-0.762344