{"id":"ma-fig-060","s":"maths","t":"s2-01","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows test marks against revision time, with the regression line of mark on time. Find the residual at point P, taking residual as observed mark minus predicted mark.","o":["$15$ marks","$2.5$ marks","$-5$ marks","$5$ marks"],"a":3,"x":"At the revision time of P, the regression line gives a predicted mark of 15. The observed mark at P is 20. Residual = observed − predicted = $20-15=5$ marks, so P lies above the line.","w":["Read the predicted mark from the regression line instead of finding the difference from the observed mark.","Used the gradient of the line, 2.5, rather than the vertical distance between P and the line.","Subtracted the wrong way round (predicted − observed), which gives the wrong sign for the residual.",""],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,8],"y":[0,30],"xstep":1,"ystep":5,"xlabel":"revision time / hours","ylabel":"test mark","lines":[{"pts":[[1,5],[2,12],[3,11],[4,20],[5,16],[6,18],[7,24],[8,24]],"dots":true,"c":0},{"pts":[[0.0,5.0],[0.05,5.125],[0.1,5.25],[0.15,5.375],[0.2,5.5],[0.25,5.625],[0.3,5.75],[0.35,5.875],[0.4,6.0],[0.45,6.125],[0.5,6.25],[0.55,6.375],[0.6,6.5],[0.65,6.625],[0.7,6.75],[0.75,6.875],[0.8,7.0],[0.85,7.125],[0.9,7.25],[0.95,7.375],[1.0,7.5],[1.05,7.625],[1.1,7.75],[1.15,7.875],[1.2,8.0],[1.25,8.125],[1.3,8.25],[1.35,8.375],[1.4,8.5],[1.45,8.625],[1.5,8.75],[1.55,8.875],[1.6,9.0],[1.65,9.125],[1.7,9.25],[1.75,9.375],[1.8,9.5],[1.85,9.625],[1.9,9.75],[1.95,9.875],[2.0,10.0],[2.05,10.125],[2.1,10.25],[2.15,10.375],[2.2,10.5],[2.25,10.625],[2.3,10.75],[2.35,10.875],[2.4,11.0],[2.45,11.125],[2.5,11.25],[2.55,11.375],[2.6,11.5],[2.65,11.625],[2.7,11.75],[2.75,11.875],[2.8,12.0],[2.85,12.125],[2.9,12.25],[2.95,12.375],[3.0,12.5],[3.05,12.625],[3.1,12.75],[3.15,12.875],[3.2,13.0],[3.25,13.125],[3.3,13.25],[3.35,13.375],[3.4,13.5],[3.45,13.625],[3.5,13.75],[3.55,13.875],[3.6,14.0],[3.65,14.125],[3.7,14.25],[3.75,14.375],[3.8,14.5],[3.85,14.625],[3.9,14.75],[3.95,14.875],[4.0,15.0],[4.05,15.125],[4.1,15.25],[4.15,15.375],[4.2,15.5],[4.25,15.625],[4.3,15.75],[4.35,15.875],[4.4,16.0],[4.45,16.125],[4.5,16.25],[4.55,16.375],[4.6,16.5],[4.65,16.625],[4.7,16.75],[4.75,16.875],[4.8,17.0],[4.85,17.125],[4.9,17.25],[4.95,17.375],[5.0,17.5],[5.05,17.625],[5.1,17.75],[5.15,17.875],[5.2,18.0],[5.25,18.125],[5.3,18.25],[5.35,18.375],[5.4,18.5],[5.45,18.625],[5.5,18.75],[5.55,18.875],[5.6,19.0],[5.65,19.125],[5.7,19.25],[5.75,19.375],[5.8,19.5],[5.85,19.625],[5.9,19.75],[5.95,19.875],[6.0,20.0],[6.05,20.125],[6.1,20.25],[6.15,20.375],[6.2,20.5],[6.25,20.625],[6.3,20.75],[6.35,20.875],[6.4,21.0],[6.45,21.125],[6.5,21.25],[6.55,21.375],[6.6,21.5],[6.65,21.625],[6.7,21.75],[6.75,21.875],[6.8,22.0],[6.85,22.125],[6.9,22.25],[6.95,22.375],[7.0,22.5],[7.05,22.625],[7.1,22.75],[7.15,22.875],[7.2,23.0],[7.25,23.125],[7.3,23.25],[7.35,23.375],[7.4,23.5],[7.45,23.625],[7.5,23.75],[7.55,23.875],[7.6,24.0],[7.65,24.125],[7.7,24.25],[7.75,24.375],[7.8,24.5],[7.85,24.625],[7.9,24.75],[7.95,24.875],[8.0,25.0]],"c":1,"label":"regression"}],"points":[{"at":[4,20],"label":"P"}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"ma-fig-048","s":"maths","t":"p2-06","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows y = cosec x for 0° < x < 360° and the dashed line y = k. Which pair of values of x solves cosec x = k in this interval?","o":["$30^\\circ$ and $150^\\circ$","$120^\\circ$ and $240^\\circ$","$210^\\circ$ and $330^\\circ$","$240^\\circ$ and $300^\\circ$"],"a":2,"x":"The dashed line is at $k=-2$. So $\\operatorname{cosec}x=-2$, giving $\\sin x=-\\frac12$. The reference angle is $30^\\circ$, with sine negative in the third and fourth quadrants: $210^\\circ$ and $330^\\circ$.","w":["Ignored that the dashed line is below the axis and used $\\sin x=+\\frac12$, giving the first and second quadrant angles.","Used $\\cos x=-\\frac12$, confusing cosec with sec, or took the reference angle as $60^\\circ$.","","Used $\\sin x=-\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}$, mixing up the exact values for $30^\\circ$ and $60^\\circ$."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,360],"y":[-4,4],"xstep":60,"ystep":1,"xlabel":"x / degrees","ylabel":"y","lines":[{"pts":[[15,3.864],[20,2.924],[25,2.366],[30,2],[40,1.556],[50,1.305],[60,1.155],[70,1.064],[80,1.015],[90,1],[100,1.015],[110,1.064],[120,1.155],[130,1.305],[140,1.556],[150,2],[155,2.366],[160,2.924],[165,3.864]],"label":"y = cosec x","c":0},{"pts":[[195,-3.864],[200,-2.924],[205,-2.366],[210,-2],[220,-1.556],[230,-1.305],[240,-1.155],[250,-1.064],[260,-1.015],[270,-1],[280,-1.015],[290,-1.064],[300,-1.155],[310,-1.305],[320,-1.556],[330,-2],[335,-2.366],[340,-2.924],[345,-3.864]],"c":1},{"pts":[[0,-2],[360,-2]],"dash":true,"label":"y = k","c":2}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"ma-fig-049","s":"maths","t":"p2-06","lv":3,"q":"The graph shows y = tan x and y = cot x for 0° < x < 180°. For which values of x is cot x > tan x?","o":["$0^\\circ<x<45^\\circ$ and $90^\\circ<x<135^\\circ$","$45^\\circ<x<90^\\circ$ and $135^\\circ<x<180^\\circ$","$0^\\circ<x<45^\\circ$ only","$0^\\circ<x<45^\\circ$ and $135^\\circ<x<180^\\circ$"],"a":0,"x":"The curves cross at P and Q, where $x=45^\\circ$ and $135^\\circ$, and tan has an asymptote at $90^\\circ$. The cot curve lies above the tan curve for $0<x<45$ and again for $90<x<135$.","w":["","Swapped the curves, giving the intervals where tan x is above cot x.","Stopped at the first crossing and missed that cot is above tan again after the asymptote of tan.","Took cot as higher where it is steeper, so chose the interval where cot is very negative and below tan."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,180],"y":[-4,4],"xstep":45,"ystep":1,"xlabel":"x / degrees","ylabel":"y","lines":[{"pts":[[15,3.732],[20,2.747],[25,2.145],[30,1.732],[35,1.428],[40,1.192],[45,1],[50,0.839],[60,0.577],[70,0.364],[80,0.176],[90,0],[100,-0.176],[110,-0.364],[120,-0.577],[130,-0.839],[135,-1],[140,-1.192],[145,-1.428],[150,-1.732],[155,-2.145],[160,-2.747],[165,-3.732]],"label":"cot x","c":0},{"pts":[[0,0],[10,0.176],[20,0.364],[25,0.466],[30,0.577],[35,0.7],[40,0.839],[45,1],[50,1.192],[60,1.732],[70,2.747],[75,3.732]],"label":"tan x","c":1},{"pts":[[105,-3.732],[110,-2.747],[120,-1.732],[130,-1.192],[135,-1],[140,-0.839],[150,-0.577],[160,-0.364],[170,-0.176],[180,0]],"c":2}],"points":[{"at":[45,1],"label":"P"},{"at":[135,-1],"label":"Q"}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"ma-fig-050","s":"maths","t":"p2-06","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows y = arcsin(kx), drawn for the whole of its domain. What is the value of k?","o":["$k=\\frac12$","$k=4$","$k=1$","$k=2$"],"a":3,"x":"$\\arcsin(kx)$ needs $-1\\le kx\\le1$. The graph ends at $x=\\pm0.5$, so $0.5k=1$ and $k=2$.","w":["Took the end of the domain, $0.5$, as $k$ instead of solving $0.5k=1$.","Used the gridline spacing $0.25$ as the end of the domain, giving $k=\\frac{1}{0.25}$.","Assumed the ordinary $\\arcsin x$, whose domain ends at $x=\\pm1$, and ignored where this curve stops.",""],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[-1,1],"y":[-2,2],"xstep":0.25,"ystep":0.5,"xlabel":"x","ylabel":"y / radians","lines":[{"pts":[[-0.5,-1.5708],[-0.495,-1.4293],[-0.475,-1.2532],[-0.45,-1.1198],[-0.4,-0.9273],[-0.35,-0.7754],[-0.3,-0.6435],[-0.25,-0.5236],[-0.2,-0.4115],[-0.1,-0.2013],[0,0],[0.1,0.2013],[0.2,0.4115],[0.25,0.5236],[0.3,0.6435],[0.35,0.7754],[0.4,0.9273],[0.45,1.1198],[0.475,1.2532],[0.495,1.4293],[0.5,1.5708]],"label":"arcsin(kx)","c":0}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"ma-fig-033","s":"maths","t":"s1-04","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows a regression line of test score on revision time. Using points P and Q on the line, which interpretation of the gradient is correct?","o":["Each extra mark needs about 5 more hours of revision, on average","Each extra hour of revision adds 7 marks, on average","Each extra hour of revision is linked to 5 more marks, on average","A student who does no revision is predicted to score 5 marks"],"a":2,"x":"P is (2, 30) and Q is (10, 70). Gradient $=\\dfrac{70-30}{10-2}=5$ marks per hour. It is an average trend linking revision time and score, not a guaranteed or causal change.","w":["Swaps the variables. The gradient is 5 marks per hour of revision, not 5 hours per mark.","Uses $70\\div10$ from point Q alone, ignoring that the line does not pass through the origin.","","Confuses the gradient with the intercept. The predicted score at 0 hours is the $y$-intercept, 20."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,10],"y":[0,80],"xstep":2,"ystep":10,"xlabel":"revision time / hours","ylabel":"test score / marks","lines":[{"pts":[[1,27],[2,33],[3,32],[4,38],[5,48],[6,47],[7,58],[8,57],[9,68],[10,66]],"dots":true,"c":0},{"pts":[[0,20],[10,70]],"c":1}],"points":[{"at":[2,30],"label":"P"},{"at":[10,70],"label":"Q"}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"ma-fig-034","s":"maths","t":"s1-04","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows the value of used cars of different ages. Describe the correlation and say what removing the labelled point P would do to it.","o":["Negative correlation; removing P would make it weaker","Positive correlation; removing P would make it stronger","Negative correlation; removing P would make no difference","Negative correlation; removing P would make it stronger"],"a":3,"x":"Value falls as age rises, so the correlation is negative. P (age 6, value 12) lies far from the downward trend of the other points. Removing it leaves the points closer to a straight line, so the correlation is stronger.","w":["Thinks the outlier supports the trend. P lies far from the pattern, so removing it strengthens the correlation.","Reads the trend the wrong way round. Value falls as age rises, which is negative correlation.","Thinks one point cannot change the correlation. An outlier clearly weakens the linear relationship.",""],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,10],"y":[0,16],"xstep":2,"ystep":2,"xlabel":"age of car / years","ylabel":"value / £ thousands","lines":[{"pts":[[1,14],[2,12],[3,11],[4,9],[5,8],[6,6],[8,4],[9,3],[10,2]],"dots":true,"c":0}],"points":[{"at":[6,12],"label":"P"}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"ma-fig-035","s":"maths","t":"s1-04","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows crop yield against fertiliser, with a regression line fitted to the plotted data. Point R lies on the dashed extension. Which statement about predicting from R is correct?","o":["Yield of 100 kg, which is reliable because the line fits the data well","Yield of 100 kg, which is unreliable because it is extrapolation","Yield of 40 kg, which is unreliable because it is extrapolation","Yield of 40 kg, which is reliable because it is interpolation"],"a":1,"x":"R is (40, 100), so the predicted yield is 100 kg. The data only cover fertiliser amounts from 10 to 30 g per m², so 40 is outside that range. This is extrapolation, which is unreliable.","w":["Reads the yield correctly but ignores that R is outside the data range. A good fit inside the range does not justify extrapolation.","","Reads the $x$ value (40) as the yield. The yield is on the vertical axis.","Reads the $x$ value as the yield and treats 40 as inside the data range. It is outside the 10 to 30 range."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,50],"y":[0,120],"xstep":10,"ystep":20,"xlabel":"fertiliser / g per m²","ylabel":"yield / kg","lines":[{"pts":[[10,38],[12,46],[15,48],[18,58],[20,58],[23,68],[25,72],[28,74],[30,82]],"dots":true,"c":0},{"pts":[[10,40],[30,80]],"c":1},{"pts":[[30,80],[50,120]],"dash":true,"c":2}],"points":[{"at":[40,100],"label":"R"}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"ma-fig-011","s":"maths","t":"p1-03","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows lines L and M meeting at P. Which pair of equations has the coordinates of P as its solution?","o":["$y=\\frac{1}{2}x+2$ and $x+y=8$","$y=2x+2$ and $y-x=8$","$y=2x+2$ and $x+y=8$","$y=2x+2$ and $x+y=6$"],"a":2,"x":"L crosses the $y$-axis at 2 and rises 2 for each 1 across, so $y=2x+2$. M passes through $(0,8)$ and $(4,4)$, so $x+y=8$. At P $(2,6)$ both equations hold.","w":["Gradient of L upside down: it rises 2 per 1 across, not 1 per 2 across.","Sign slip for M: it slopes downwards (gradient $-1$), but $y-x=8$ slopes upwards.","","Used the $y$-coordinate of P, 6, as the constant for M instead of M's intercept 8. The point $(2,6)$ does not satisfy $x+y=6$."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[-1,5],"y":[0,10],"xstep":1,"ystep":2,"xlabel":"x","ylabel":"y","lines":[{"pts":[[-1,0],[4,10]],"label":"L","c":0},{"pts":[[-1,9],[5,3]],"label":"M","c":1}],"points":[{"at":[2,6],"label":"P"}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"ma-fig-012","s":"maths","t":"p1-03","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows $y=f(x)$, where $f(x)=x^2-4x+2$, and the dashed line $y=k$. Which statement about the equation $f(x)=k$ is correct?","o":["One repeated solution: the line is horizontal, so $b^2-4ac=0$","No real solutions: the line is below the minimum point, so $b^2-4ac<0$","Two real solutions: the line is below the $x$-axis, so $b^2-4ac>0$","Two real solutions: the curve crosses the $x$-axis, so $b^2-4ac>0$"],"a":1,"x":"The minimum point of the curve is $(2,-2)$. The dashed line is at $y=-4$, below it, so it never meets the curve. Then $x^2-4x+2-k=0$ has no real roots, so $b^2-4ac<0$.","w":["Thought a horizontal line must touch the curve once. Only the line through the minimum point does that.","","Thought that being below the $x$-axis gives two roots. The line must be compared with the curve's minimum point.","Confused $f(x)=k$ with $f(x)=0$. The roots of $f(x)=k$ come from the line $y=k$, not the $x$-axis."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[-1,5],"y":[-6,8],"xstep":1,"ystep":2,"xlabel":"x","ylabel":"y","lines":[{"pts":[[-1.0,7.0],[-0.9625,6.776406],[-0.925,6.555625],[-0.8875,6.337656],[-0.85,6.1225],[-0.8125,5.910156],[-0.775,5.700625],[-0.7375,5.493906],[-0.7,5.29],[-0.6625,5.088906],[-0.625,4.890625],[-0.5875,4.695156],[-0.55,4.5025],[-0.5125,4.312656],[-0.475,4.125625],[-0.4375,3.941406],[-0.4,3.76],[-0.3625,3.581406],[-0.325,3.405625],[-0.2875,3.232656],[-0.25,3.0625],[-0.2125,2.895156],[-0.175,2.730625],[-0.1375,2.568906],[-0.1,2.41],[-0.0625,2.253906],[-0.025,2.100625],[0.0125,1.950156],[0.05,1.8025],[0.0875,1.657656],[0.125,1.515625],[0.1625,1.376406],[0.2,1.24],[0.2375,1.106406],[0.275,0.975625],[0.3125,0.847656],[0.35,0.7225],[0.3875,0.600156],[0.425,0.480625],[0.4625,0.363906],[0.5,0.25],[0.5375,0.138906],[0.575,0.030625],[0.6125,-0.074844],[0.65,-0.1775],[0.6875,-0.277344],[0.725,-0.374375],[0.7625,-0.468594],[0.8,-0.56],[0.8375,-0.648594],[0.875,-0.734375],[0.9125,-0.817344],[0.95,-0.8975],[0.9875,-0.97