{"id":"ma-fig-038","s":"maths","t":"m1-09","lv":2,"q":"A car brakes with constant deceleration. The graph shows its velocity during the first part of the braking. If the deceleration stays the same, find the total distance the car travels before it stops.","o":["100 m","64 m","400 m","50 m"],"a":0,"x":"Gradient $=(12-20)/4=-2$, so $a=-2$ m/s² and $u=20$ m/s. Using $v^2=u^2+2as$: $0=400-4s$, so $s=100$ m.","w":["","Finds only the distance covered in the 4 s shown: $\\frac12(20+12)\\times4=64$ m.","Turns the gradient upside down ($a=0.5$), giving $400\\div1=400$ m.","Uses a deceleration of 4 (the fall in speed, not per second), giving $400\\div8=50$ m."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,6],"y":[0,24],"xstep":1,"ystep":4,"xlabel":"time / s","ylabel":"velocity / m/s","lines":[{"pts":[[0,20],[4,12]],"c":0}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"ma-fig-063","s":"maths","t":"m2-08","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows the i- and j-components of the velocity of a particle moving with constant acceleration. Use it to find the magnitude of the acceleration of the particle.","o":["$7\\text{ m s}^{-2}$","$1\\text{ m s}^{-2}$","$5\\text{ m s}^{-2}$","$8.9\\text{ m s}^{-2}$"],"a":2,"x":"Acceleration components are the gradients. i-component: $(-4-8)/4=-3$. j-component: $(12-(-4))/4=4$. Magnitude $=\\sqrt{(-3)^2+4^2}=5\\text{ m s}^{-2}$.","w":["Added the gradient sizes 3 and 4 instead of using Pythagoras on the components.","Added the signed gradients, $-3+4$, instead of finding the magnitude of the vector.","","Found the initial speed from the two $t=0$ intercepts rather than the gradients."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,4],"y":[-8,12],"xstep":1,"ystep":4,"xlabel":"time / s","ylabel":"velocity component / m/s","lines":[{"pts":[[0,8],[4,-4]],"label":"i-comp","c":0},{"pts":[[0,-4],[4,12]],"label":"j-comp","c":1}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"ma-fig-064","s":"maths","t":"m2-08","lv":3,"q":"A particle has initial velocity $(-5\\mathbf{i}+4\\mathbf{j})\\text{ m s}^{-1}$. Its acceleration has no j-component, and the graph shows its i-component. Find the speed of the particle when $t=4$ s.","o":["$11.7\\text{ m s}^{-1}$","$6.4\\text{ m s}^{-1}$","$8.9\\text{ m s}^{-1}$","$5\\text{ m s}^{-1}$"],"a":3,"x":"Change in $v_x$ is the area under the graph: $\\frac12\\times4\\times4=8$. So $v_x=-5+8=3$. Since $a_y=0$, $v_y=4$. Speed $=\\sqrt{3^2+4^2}=5\\text{ m s}^{-1}$.","w":["Treated the acceleration as constant at its $t=0$ value, giving an area of 16 instead of 8, so $v_x=11$.","Ignored the area under the graph, leaving $v_x=-5$ and speed $\\sqrt{41}$.","Used the area, 8, as $v_x$ and forgot to add the initial $-5$, giving $\\sqrt{8^2+4^2}$.",""],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,4],"y":[0,5],"xstep":1,"ystep":1,"xlabel":"time / s","ylabel":"i-component of acceleration / m/s²","lines":[{"pts":[[0,4],[4,0]],"c":0}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"ma-fig-051","s":"maths","t":"p2-08","lv":2,"q":"A curve has parametric equations $x=t^2$, $y=t^3-4t$. The graph shows the curve and the point P on it. Find the gradient of the curve at P.","o":["$-2$","$\\frac{1}{2}$","$-\\frac{1}{2}$","$2$"],"a":2,"x":"P is at $(1,-3)$. $x=1$ gives $t=\\pm1$, and $y=-3$ gives $t=1$. $\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{3t^2-4}{2t}$, so at $t=1$ the gradient is $\\frac{-1}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}$.","w":["Uses $\\frac{dx}{dy}$ upside down, $\\frac{2t}{3t^2-4}$, which is the reciprocal of the gradient.","Takes $t=-1$ instead of $t=1$ because $x=1$ was read without using the $y$-coordinate of P.","","Takes $t=-1$ and also inverts the fraction. Both slips together give $2$."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,5],"y":[-4,4],"xstep":1,"ystep":1,"xlabel":"x","ylabel":"y","lines":[{"pts":[[4.84,-1.848],[4,0],[3.24,1.368],[2.56,2.304],[1.96,2.856],[1.44,3.072],[1,3],[0.64,2.688],[0.36,2.184],[0.16,1.536],[0.04,0.792],[0.01,0.399],[0,0],[0.01,-0.399],[0.04,-0.792],[0.16,-1.536],[0.36,-2.184],[0.64,-2.688],[1,-3],[1.44,-3.072],[1.96,-2.856],[2.56,-2.304],[3.24,-1.368],[4,0],[4.84,1.848]],"c":0}],"points":[{"at":[1,-3],"label":"P"}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"ma-fig-052","s":"maths","t":"p2-08","lv":2,"q":"A ball is hit from the ground. Its horizontal position is modelled by $x=6t$, with $t$ in seconds. The graph shows its path. How long after being hit is the ball at its greatest height?","o":["$4$ s","$2$ s","$12$ s","$3.3$ s"],"a":1,"x":"The greatest height is at the top of the path, where $x=12$ m. Since $x=6t$, $t=\\frac{12}{6}=2$ s.","w":["Reads the landing point $x=24$ and divides by 6. That is the total time of flight, not the time to the top.","","Reads the $x$-value at the top, 12, as if it were the time. It must be divided by 6.","Divides the maximum height, 20, by 6. The horizontal position at the top should be used."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,24],"y":[0,24],"xstep":4,"ystep":4,"xlabel":"horizontal distance x / m","ylabel":"height y / m","lines":[{"pts":[[0.0,0.0],[0.15,0.496875],[0.3,0.9875],[0.45,1.471875],[0.6,1.95],[0.75,2.421875],[0.9,2.8875],[1.05,3.346875],[1.2,3.8],[1.35,4.246875],[1.5,4.6875],[1.65,5.121875],[1.8,5.55],[1.95,5.971875],[2.1,6.3875],[2.25,6.796875],[2.4,7.2],[2.55,7.596875],[2.7,7.9875],[2.85,8.371875],[3.0,8.75],[3.15,9.121875],[3.3,9.4875],[3.45,9.846875],[3.6,10.2],[3.75,10.546875],[3.9,10.8875],[4.05,11.221875],[4.2,11.55],[4.35,11.871875],[4.5,12.1875],[4.65,12.496875],[4.8,12.8],[4.95,13.096875],[5.1,13.3875],[5.25,13.671875],[5.4,13.95],[5.55,14.221875],[5.7,14.4875],[5.85,14.746875],[6.0,15.0],[6.15,15.246875],[6.3,15.4875],[6.45,15.721875],[6.6,15.95],[6.75,16.171875],[6.9,16.3875],[7.05,16.596875],[7.2,16.8],[7.35,16.996875],[7.5,17.1875],[7.65,17.371875],[7.8,17.55],[7.95,17.721875],[8.1,17.8875],[8.25,18.046875],[8.4,18.2],[8.55,18.346875],[8.7,18.4875],[8.85,18.621875],[9.0,18.75],[9.15,18.871875],[9.3,18.9875],[9.45,19.096875],[9.6,19.2],[9.75,19.296875],[9.9,19.3875],[10.05,19.471875],[10.2,19.55],[10.35,19.621875],[10.5,19.6875],[10.65,19.746875],[10.8,19.8],[10.95,19.846875],[11.1,19.8875],[11.25,19.921875],[11.4,19.95],[11.55,19.971875],[11.7,19.9875],[11.8