{"id":"ma-fig-073","s":"maths","t":"p2-04","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows $y=(2+3x)^{-2}$ and its expansion up to $x^3$. Use it to find the constant term, and decide why the curves separate towards the left of the graph.","o":["$\\frac12$; the expansion is only valid for $|x|<\\frac13$, so it fails here","$\\frac14$; the expansion is only valid for $|x|<\\frac13$, so it fails here","$\\frac14$; the expansion is valid here, but leaving out $x^4$ and later terms causes the gap","$\\frac12$; the expansion is valid here, but leaving out $x^4$ and later terms causes the gap"],"a":2,"x":"Both curves pass through P at $y=0.25$, so the constant term is $\\frac14$. Validity is $|\\frac{3x}{2}|<1$, so $|x|<\\frac23$, which includes the left edge. The gap comes from the omitted $x^4$ and later terms.","w":["Takes the constant term as $\\frac12$ instead of reading 0.25, and uses the validity condition $|3x|<1$, forgetting the factor of 2.","Reads the intercept correctly but uses $|3x|<1$, forgetting to factor out the 2, so wrongly thinks $x=-0.5$ is outside the valid range.","","Takes the constant term as $2^{-1}=\\frac12$ instead of $2^{-2}$; the intercept at P on the graph is 0.25, not 0.5."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[-0.5,0.5],"y":[-0.5,1.5],"xstep":0.25,"ystep":0.25,"xlabel":"x","ylabel":"y","lines":[{"pts":[[-0.5,4.0],[-0.49375,3.716069],[-0.4875,3.46133],[-0.48125,3.231915],[-0.475,3.024575],[-0.46875,2.836565],[-0.4625,2.665556],[-0.45625,2.509558],[-0.45,2.366864],[-0.44375,2.236003],[-0.4375,2.115702],[-0.43125,2.004856],[-0.425,1.902497],[-0.41875,1.807782],[-0.4125,1.719968],[-0.40625,1.6384],[-0.4,1.5625],[-0.39375,1.491755],[-0.3875,1.425707],[-0.38125,1.363951],[-0.375,1.306122],[-0.36875,1.251895],[-0.3625,1.200976],[-0.35625,1.153101],[-0.35,1.108033],[-0.34375,1.065557],[-0.3375,1.025477],[-0.33125,0.987616],[-0.325,0.951814],[-0.31875,0.917925],[-0.3125,0.885813],[-0.30625,0.855358],[-0.3,0.826446],[-0.29375,0.798976],[-0.2875,0.772854],[-0.28125,0.747991],[-0.2