{"id":"ma-fig-076","s":"maths","t":"s1-03","lv":2,"q":"The graph is a histogram of journey times for a sample of commuters. How many of the journeys took more than 20 minutes?","o":["15","2","35","20"],"a":3,"x":"Frequency = frequency density × class width. From the graph, 20–30 minutes: 1.5 × 10 = 15. 30–40 minutes: 0.5 × 10 = 5. Total = 15 + 5 = 20.","w":["Used only the 20–30 minute bar and left out the 30–40 minute bar.","Added the frequency densities (1.5 + 0.5) instead of multiplying each by its class width.","Included the 15–20 minute class, which is not above 20 minutes.",""],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,40],"y":[0,3.5],"xstep":5,"ystep":0.5,"xlabel":"journey time / minutes","ylabel":"frequency density","lines":[{"pts":[[0,0],[0,1],[10,1],[10,2],[15,2],[15,3],[20,3],[20,1.5],[30,1.5],[30,0.5],[40,0.5],[40,0],[0,0]],"c":0}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"ma-fig-077","s":"maths","t":"s1-03","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows box plots of journey times for a bus route and a train route. Which statement about the two routes is supported by the graph?","o":["The train has the higher median and the smaller interquartile range","The bus has the higher median because its box is wider","The train has the higher median and the larger interquartile range","The medians are equal because both boxes end at the same upper quartile"],"a":0,"x":"From the graph, the train median is 35 minutes and the bus median is 25 minutes, so the train is higher. The train IQR is 40 − 30 = 10 and the bus IQR is 40 − 20 = 20, so the train is smaller.","w":["","Confused spread with average: a wider box means a larger IQR, not a higher median.","Read the medians correctly but took the bus's longer box as the train's.","Read the shared upper quartile as the median. The median is the line inside each box."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[10,55],"y":[0,4],"xstep":5,"ystep":1,"xlabel":"journey time / minutes","ylabel":"route","lines":[{"label":"Bus","pts":[[15,3],[20,3],[20,3.5],[40,3.5],[40,2.5],[20,2.5],[20,3.5],[25,3.5],[25,2.5],[40,2.5],[40,3],[50,3]],"c":0},{"label":"Train","pts":[[25,1],[30,1],[30,1.5],[40,1.5],[40,0.5],[30,0.5],[30,1.5],[35,1.5],[35,0.5],[40,0.5],[40,1],[45,1]],"c":1}],"xnums":true,"ynums":false}}
{"id":"ma-fig-070","s":"maths","t":"p1-12","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows a curve and its gradient function, labelled A and B. Which option gives the coordinates of the local minimum point of the original curve?","o":["$(0, 0)$","$(2, 0)$","$(2, -4)$","$(-4, 2)$"],"a":2,"x":"B crosses the x-axis where A has its turning points, so B is the gradient function and A is the original curve. A's turning points are at $x=0$ and $x=2$. The minimum is at $x=2$, and A has $y=-4$ there.","w":["Gives the local maximum of A, not the minimum. The curve is at a peak at $x=0$ and a trough at $x=2$.","Takes the y-value from the gradient curve B, which is 0 at a stationary point, instead of the y-value on the original curve A.","","Swaps the coordinates, writing $(y, x)$ instead of $(x, y)$."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[-1,3],"y":[-6,10],"xstep":1,"ystep":2,"xlabel":"x","ylabel":"y","lines":[{"pts":[[-1.0,-4.0],[-0.975,-3.778734],[-0.95,-3.564875],[-0.925,-3.358328],[-0.9,-3.159],[-0.875,-2.966797],[-0.85,-2.781625],[-0.825,-2.603391],[-0.8,-2.432],[-0.775,-2.267359],[-0.75,-2.109375],[-0.725,-1.957953],[-0.7,-1.813],[-0.675,-1.674422],[-0.65,-1.542125],[-0.625,-1.416016],[-0.6,-1.296],[-0.575,-1.181984],[-0.55,-1.073875],[-0.525,-0.971578],[-0.5,-0.875],[-0.475,-0.784047],[-0.45,-0.698625],[-0.425,-0.618641],[-0.4,-0.544],[-0.375,-0.474609],[-0.35,-0.410375],[-0.325,-0.351203],[-0.3,-0.297],[-0.275,-0.247672],[-0.25,-0.203125],[-0.225,-0.163266],[-0.2,-0.128],[-0.175,-0.097234],[-0.15,-0.070875],[-0.125,-0.048828],[-0.1,-0.031],[-0.075,-0.017297],[-0.05,-0.007625],[-0.025,-0.001891],[0.0,0.0],[0.025,-0.001859],[0.05,-0.007375],[0.075,-0.016453],[0.1,-0.029],[0.125,-0.044922],[0.15,-0.064125],[0.175,-0.086516],[0.2,-0.112],[0.225,-0.140484],[0.25,-0.171875],[0.275,-0.206078],[0.3,-0.243],[0.325,-0.282547],[0.35,-0.324625],[0.375,-0.369141],[0.4,-0.416],[0.425,-0.465109],[0.45,-0.516375],[0.475,-0.569703],[0.5,-0.625],[0.525,-0.682172],[0.55,-0.741125],[0.575,-0.801766],[0.6,-0.864],[0.62