{"id":"ma-fig-071","s":"maths","t":"p1-13","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows $y=x^2-4x$ and two dashed vertical lines. Which expression gives the area of the region between the curve, the $x$-axis and the two dashed lines?","o":["$\\int_1^3 (x^2-4x)\\,dx$","$-\\int_0^4 (x^2-4x)\\,dx$","$-\\int_1^3 (x^2-4x)\\,dx$","$\\int_{-3}^{0} (x^2-4x)\\,dx$"],"a":2,"x":"The dashed lines are at $x=1$ and $x=3$, so these are the limits. The curve lies below the $x$-axis there, so the integral is negative. The area is $-\\int_1^3 (x^2-4x)\\,dx=\\frac{22}{3}$.","w":["Uses the correct limits but forgets that the curve is below the axis, so the integral is negative and would not give an area.","Uses the places where the curve meets the $x$-axis ($0$ and $4$) as limits instead of the dashed lines at the region's edges.","","Uses the $y$-values at the ends of the dashed lines ($-3$ and $0$) as limits, but integrating with respect to $x$ needs $x$-limits."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[-1,5],"y":[-4,5],"xstep":1,"ystep":1,"xlabel":"x","ylabel":"y","lines":[{"pts":[[-1.0,5.0],[-0.9625,4.776406],[-0.925,4.555625],[-0.8875,4.337656],[-0.85,4.1225],[-0.8125,3.910156],[-0.775,3.700625],[-0.7375,3.493906],[-0.7,3.29],[-0.6625,3.088906],[-0.625,2.890625],[-0.5875,2.695156],[-0.55,2.5025],[-0.5125,2.312656],[-0.475,2.125625],[-0.4375,1.941406],[-0.4,1.76],[-0.3625,1.581406],[-0.325,1.405625],[-0.2875,1.232656],[-0.25,1.0625],[-0.2125,0.895156],[-0.175,0.730625],[-0.1375,0.568906],[-0.1,0.41],[-0.0625,0.253906],[-0.025,0.100625],[0.0125,-0.049844],[0.05,-0.1975],[0.0875,-0.342344],[0.125,-0.484375],[0.1625,-0.623594],[0.2,-0.76],[0.2375,-0.893594],[0.275,-1.024375],[0.3125,-1.152344],[0.35,-1.2775],[0.3875,-1.399844],[0.425,-1.519375],[0.4625,-1.636094],[0.5,-1.75],[0.5375,-1.861094],[0.575,-1.969375],[0.6125,-2.074844],[0.65,-2.1775],[0.6875,-2.277344],[0.725,-2.374375],[0.7625,-2.468594],[0.8,-2.56],[0.8375,-2.648594],[0.875,-2.734375],[0.9125,-2.817344],[0.95,-2.8975],[0.9875,-2.974844],[1.025,-3.0493